WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.750
A student trains a cleaning agent rewarded

2
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:05.810
for each piece of dirt it picks up.

3
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.380
It learns to drop dirt and

4
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:09.940
pick it up again, forever.

5
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:11.920
The code has no bug.

6
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:13.560
What went wrong?

7
00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:17.490
Today: the classical definition of an

8
00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.810
agent, how Markov decision processes model

9
00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.950
sequential choices, and why reward design

10
00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:26.110
is where student projects go wrong.

11
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.890
An agent perceives its environment through

12
00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.750
sensors and acts on it through actuators.

13
00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.060
A rational agent chooses actions that

14
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.520
maximise its expected performance measure.

15
00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.660
PEAS names the four things to

16
00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.200
specify: performance,

17
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.440
environment, actuators and sensors.

18
00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.710
An MDP has states, actions,

19
00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:53.770
transition probabilities and rewards.

20
00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:57.290
A policy maps each state to an action.

21
00:00:57.990 --> 00:00:59.810
The goal is the policy that

22
00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:02.530
maximises expected discounted return.

23
00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.700
With a discount of zero point nine, a

24
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.860
reward of ten arriving in two steps is

25
00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.400
worth eight point one now.

26
00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:13.460
Discounting makes sooner

27
00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:14.820
rewards worth more.

28
00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:18.170
It also keeps infinite sums finite.

29
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.660
The agent optimises exactly the reward

30
00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:24.800
you wrote, not the goal you meant.

31
00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.490
Rewarding pickups instead of a

32
00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:29.730
clean room invites the loop.

33
00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.740
This is called specification gaming,

34
00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:34.740
and it happens in real systems.

35
00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.470
Students often think an agent

36
00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.290
understands its task.

37
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.660
Give them a small gridworld and let

38
00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:46.760
them design the reward themselves.

39
00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:50.210
Then show them what their agent actually

40
00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:53.530
learned, and let them fix the reward.

41
00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:55.340
Quick check!

42
00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.360
With a discount of zero point five,

43
00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.040
what is a reward of eight, arriving

44
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.740
in three steps, worth now?

45
00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:07.390
One: zero point five cubed is one

46
00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:10.210
eighth, and one eighth of eight is one.

47
00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.500
A low discount makes the

48
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:13.800
agent short-sighted.

49
00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.720
In the app, open the agent loop lab.

50
00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:21.000
Watch Darsi Tiny take a request one

51
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.040
step at a time: its plan, the tool

52
00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.220
call, the result and its answer.

53
00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.560
Then name its PEAS: its performance

54
00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.400
measure, environment, actuators and

55
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:33.760
sensors.

56
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:35.840
Your turn.

57
00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.040
Which reward best stops the cleaning agent

58
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.900
from dropping and re-collecting dirt?

59
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.460
Reward the state of the room, such as how

60
00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.900
clean it is, rather than each action.

61
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:54.840
Remember: specify PEAS first, MDPs model

62
00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.040
sequential decisions, and the agent

63
00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.340
optimises exactly what you reward.

64
00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.420
Try it in your next lecture: give pairs a

65
00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.980
paper gridworld and ask them to write a

66
00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:07.440
reward for reaching the exit.

67
00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.950
Swap rewards between pairs, and let each

68
00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:13.150
pair find a loophole in the other's

69
00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:13.490
design.
